course: everyday
lesson: 263
title: Clocks, calendars and planning the day
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: On an analogue clock (a clock with hands) the short hand shows the hour and the long hand shows the minutes; each number on the face stands for 5 minutes, so the long hand on 4 means 20 minutes past.
explain: With daylight saving time, clocks go forward one hour in spring and back one hour in autumn: «spring forward, fall back»; because the seasons are opposite south of the equator, Australia and New Zealand change on different dates from the USA and the UK.
explain: In Australia and New Zealand summer is December to February, so Christmas is in summer; and the date 3/4 means 3 April in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, but March 4 in the USA.
explain: Sunset changes with the seasons, so «be home by dusk» means much earlier in winter; a timer or alarm stops you losing track of time while playing.
explain: A girl in Belfast plans her free Saturday: jobs first, then fun; she times her room tidying once and learns it takes 25 minutes, not the 5 she guessed.
fact: On an analogue clock the short hand shows the hour, the long hand shows the minutes, and each number means 5 minutes.
fact: For daylight saving time clocks go forward one hour in spring and back one hour in autumn: «spring forward, fall back».
fact: The northern and southern hemispheres change their clocks on different dates.
fact: «Thirty days has September, April, June and November»; February has 28 days, or 29 in a leap year (every fourth year), and the other months have 31.
fact: In Australia and New Zealand summer is December to February, and Christmas is in summer.
fact: In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand 3/4 means 3 April; in the USA it means March 4.
fact: A family calendar shows everyone what is planned, so events do not clash.
fact: Australia and New Zealand have four school terms a year, the UK and Ireland three, and many US schools two semesters.
fact: A timer or alarm reminds you when you must go home.
fact: The sun sets much earlier in winter than in summer, so «home by dusk» (home by the time it gets dark) changes with the seasons.
fact: In a free day, doing jobs first lets you enjoy the fun without worry.
fact: Most people think jobs are quicker than they really are, so timing them once helps you plan.
fact: Boredom often leads to creativity: you can read, draw, build, play outside or help at home.
exercise: On an analogue clock, which hand shows the minutes? | The long hand | The short hand | Both hands together | The thin red hand only
exercise: The long hand points to 6. How many minutes past the hour is it? | 30 minutes | 6 minutes | 60 minutes | 15 minutes
exercise: The short hand is on 3 and the long hand is on 12. What time is it? | 3 o'clock | 12:15 | Quarter past 12 | 12:03
exercise: What happens to clocks in spring for daylight saving time? | They go forward one hour | They go back one hour | They stop for one day | They go forward one day
exercise: Which months have 30 days? | September, April, June and November | January, March, May, July and August | Only February | December, August, October and July
exercise: When is summer in New Zealand? | December to February | June to August | March to May | All year round
exercise: A letter in Britain is dated 3/4. What date does it mean? | 3 April | March 4 | 3 o'clock on the 4th | 4 March
exercise: How would an American write 3 April as numbers? | 4/3 | 3/4 | 3/3 | 4/4
exercise: Your football game is next Saturday. Where should you write it at home? | On the family calendar | On your hand in pen, so you see it | Nowhere; just remember it | On a scrap of paper in your pocket
exercise: How many school terms does a year have in Australia? | Four | Three | Two | Six
exercise: You are playing at a friend's house and must be home at 5. How can you avoid forgetting? | Set a timer or an alarm on a clock | Trust yourself to remember | Wait until your mum phones | Watch for the street lights
exercise: You are told to be home by dusk in December in Michigan. What should you check? | When the sun sets that day | What time dusk was in July | How long the game lasts | When your friend's dinner is
exercise: You have a free Saturday with homework and a trip to the pool. What is a good plan? | Homework first, then the pool | The pool first, homework if there is time | Neither, to keep the day free | Homework late on Sunday night
exercise: You think washing up takes 5 minutes, but it always makes you late. What helps? | Time yourself once to see how long it really takes | Guess a shorter time next time | Wash up faster and break things | Never wash up again, so it can never make you late for anything
exercise: It is a rainy Sunday and you feel bored. What could you do? | Read, draw, build or help at home | Complain all day until it stops | Watch the rain for hours doing nothing | Ask to be driven to a shop
exercise: How many days does February have in a leap year? | 29 | 28 | 30 | 31
test: In March a family in New York forgets to put the clocks forward. What happens on Monday morning? | They arrive an hour late | They arrive an hour early | They arrive on time | They arrive a day late
test: A boy in Melbourne tells his pen pal in London that he will spend Christmas at the beach. Why is that normal for him? | December is summer in Australia | Australians move Christmas to June | Australian beaches are heated in winter | It is always winter at Christmas everywhere
test: A boy in Toronto reads an email from his aunt in Sydney: "Party on 5/6". When is the party? | 5 June | May 6 | 6 May | 5 May
test: The short hand is between 7 and 8, and the long hand points to 9. What time is it? | 7:45 | 9:35 | 8:45 | 7:09
test: A girl in Scotland is told to be home by dusk in both June and December. Why must she come home earlier in December? | The sun sets much earlier in winter | Her parents are much stricter in the winter months | Dusk always comes at 5 o'clock | The clocks stop in December
test: Your mum and dad both planned something for Saturday afternoon, and it clashes with your party. What could have prevented this? | Writing all events on a family calendar | Having more Saturdays | Not telling anyone about plans | Keeping plans in your head
test: How many days are there in November? | 30 | 31 | 28 | 29
test: A boy plays video games all Saturday morning and then worries all afternoon about his unfinished homework. What would have made his day better? | Doing the homework first, then playing without worry | Playing even longer to forget the homework | Never doing homework at weekends | Doing the homework at midnight, when the house is quiet and nobody can disturb him
test: A boy in Florida writes his birthday, 12 July, as 7/12. His cousin in England reads it as what date? | 7 December | 12 July | 7 July | 12 December
test: In autumn the clocks "fall back". What does that mean? | Clocks go back one hour | Clocks go forward one hour | Clocks fall off the wall | Clocks stop for the winter
